rats in this midterm environment. Markets and Iowa's -6 partisan lean temper that, so the forecast lands just barely on the Democratic side of dead even.", "watch": "Fuel prices and the Iran war's salience in the final weeks could either deepen the anti-GOP environment or allow Hinson to reframe the race around national security.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Joni Ernst (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's Senate seat is open with Joni Ernst retiring, and the contest between Republican Ashley Hinson and Democrat Josh Turek sits at the center of the midterm map. Most expert raters call it a toss-up, though a couple tilt Republican and one leans Democratic. Polling averages show Turek narrowly ahead, with a recent Marist poll giving him an eight-point edge, while Republican-affiliated pollsters show a tighter or Hinson-favored race. Prediction markets still give Hinson a slight edge, around 57 to 43. Hinson holds a clear cash-on-hand advantage, but Democratic outside groups have heavily outspent Republican ones on the air. The race unfolds in a state that leans Republican by six points but in a national environment currently working against the GOP, with fuel prices and the Iran conflict as live issues.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6012, "margin": 1.25}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5782, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5857, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.5846, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4983, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.503, "margin": 0.5}]}